  "jpMakeTenTitle": "Parts of ten (ikutsu to ikutsu) — Make Ten",
  "@jpMakeTenTitle": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpMakeTenBody": "Every number to ten is two parts: 7 is 3 and 4. At home: show 7 fingers and ask how many more make 10.",
  "@jpMakeTenBody": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpCherryTitle": "The cherry method (sakuranbo keisan) — Cherry Sums",
  "@jpCherryTitle": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpCherryBody": "To add 8 + 5, make ten first: split 5 into 2 and 3; 8 + 2 = 10, then 10 + 3 = 13. At home: fill a ten with buttons, then count the rest.",
  "@jpCherryBody": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpTapeTitle": "Tape diagrams (tēpu-zu) — Tape Stories",
  "@jpTapeTitle": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpTapeBody": "A story problem drawn as one tape: two parts under the whole. At home: tell a small story problem and cut a paper strip into its two parts.",
  "@jpTapeBody": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpPathTitle": "Number paths, like sugoroku (sugoroku) — Frog Hops",
  "@jpPathTitle": {
    "description": "Grown-ups: Japanese math methods"
  },
  "jpPathBody": "Moving along a numbered path, saying each number, builds the number line in the mind. At home: play a number board game to 10 or 20.",
